  icon: {fileID: 0}
  title: Quick Start — UI Canvas
  sections:
  - heading: How to Add a Progress Bar
  - : UI Canvas
    text: "How to add a progress bar\n\n1. Create the Material\n\u2022 In your Project
      window, create a new Material.[[IMAGE:0]]\n\u2022 Set the shader to:\n\n CupOHappiness/Procedural
      Progress Bar Canvas\n\n[[IMAGE:1]]\n[[IMAGE:2]]\n\n2. Add a UI Image\n\u2022
      In your Canvas, add a new UI \u2192 Image object.\n\u2022 Assign the material
      you just created to this image.\n[[IMAGE:3]]\n[[IMAGE:4]]\n\n3. Set Image Size
      and Scale\n\u2022 Set the Width and Height to be equal (e.g., 800x800).\\\n 
      This is important to avoid stretching artifacts.\n\u2022 Keep the Scale at
      (1, 1, 1).\\\n  You can adjust the bar\u2019s vertical thickness later via
      the shader properties.\n[[IMAGE:5]]\n\n4. Attach the Script\n\u2022 Add the
      ProceduralProgressBar script to the same GameObject.\n[[IMAGE:6]]\n\n5. Assign
      the Material in the Script\n\u2022 In the script component, assign the material:\n\u2022
      Either click \u201CGet Material\u201D, or drag your custom material manually.\n[[IMAGE:7]]\n\n6.
      You\u2019re now ready to experiment with fill settings, visuals, and shader
      customizations.\n\nTo test:\n\u2022 Adjust the Fill Amount property to see
      the bar animate.\n[[IMAGE:8]]\n\n\u2022 Or click the built-in Testing Buttons
      provided in the script for quick fill previews (only during runtime).\n[[IMAGE:9]]
      \n\n\n\n\n7. Optional: Enable Help Boxes\n\u2022 Keep the \u201CEnable Help
      Boxes\u201D option checked if you\u2019d like in-editor tooltips and guidance.\n[[IMAGE:10]]"
